INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A technical designer specifying seam construction for a high-stretch athletic legging chooses a 4-thread OVERLOCK seam (mock safety stitch, combining an overedge stitch with a straight safety chain) over a simpler 3-thread overlock for the main inseam. What SPECIFIC functional advantage does the 4th thread (the extra straight-line safety stitch) provide that directly matters for THIS specific high-stress, high-stretch application?', '["The 4th thread exists purely for decorative appearance with zero functional/structural contribution to seam performance","The extra straight safety-stitch thread acts as a structural backup/reinforcement — if the overedge (looper) threads are somehow cut or abraded through wear, the separate straight safety stitch still holds the seam together, preventing the complete seam failure/separation that could occur if only the 3-thread overlock''s interdependent looper threads were relied upon, which is especially important for a high-stress, high-movement application like athletic leggings where seam failure has serious functional consequences","3-thread and 4-thread overlock seams provide mathematically identical stretch and strength performance, making the choice between them purely a matter of thread cost with zero functional difference","The 4th thread''s sole function is to add a contrast color detail, unrelated to any structural or safety consideration"]'::jsonb, 'The extra straight safety-stitch thread acts as a structural backup/reinforcement — if the overedge (looper) threads are somehow cut or abraded through wear, the separate straight safety stitch still holds the seam together, preventing the complete seam failure/separation that could occur if only the 3-thread overlock''s interdependent looper threads were relied upon, which is especially important for a high-stress, high-movement application like athletic leggings where seam failure has serious functional consequences', 'This extends the earlier overlock-stitch discussion with a specific structural engineering rationale for the 4-thread variant — a 3-thread overlock''s strength relies entirely on its interlooped structure remaining intact; if abrasion or a cut damages the looper threads, the whole seam can unravel, while the 4-thread version''s additional independent straight safety-stitch line provides true structural REDUNDANCY (a backup holding mechanism), directly relevant for high-stress, failure-consequential applications like athletic leggings where a seam blowout during use is a serious functional/safety failure, not just a cosmetic defect.', 3, '{"4-thread-overlock-safety"}'::text[]),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A pattern engineer grading a fitted, princess-seamed dress from a base size Medium notices that a naive UNIFORM percentage scale-up (simply enlarging the entire pattern by a fixed %) would distort the princess seam''s curve SHAPE at larger sizes, not just its overall size. Why does princess-seam curve shape require MORE sophisticated, POINT-SPECIFIC grading rules than a simple garment with straight side seams and darts?', '["Princess seams require IDENTICAL grading treatment to simple straight side-seam garments, making this concern about curve-shape distortion technically unfounded","Since a princess seam''s curve shape encodes specific body-contour information (bust, waist, hip curvature) that doesn''t scale uniformly across a size range (as established in the earlier grade-rule discussion — different body dimensions grow at different rates), a uniform scale-up would distort the curve''s proportions relative to how a LARGER body''s actual contours differ from the base size — point-specific grading (moving each control point of the curve by its own calculated amount, based on real anthropometric progression data) is needed to keep the curve correctly shaped/proportioned at every size, not just correctly SIZED overall","Curve-based seams like princess seams are mathematically simpler to grade than straight seams, requiring LESS sophisticated grading treatment, the opposite of the actual relationship","Pattern grading rules are identical for every single seam type in a garment regardless of whether that seam is straight or curved, making princess-seam-specific consideration entirely unnecessary"]'::jsonb, 'Since a princess seam''s curve shape encodes specific body-contour information (bust, waist, hip curvature) that doesn''t scale uniformly across a size range (as established in the earlier grade-rule discussion — different body dimensions grow at different rates), a uniform scale-up would distort the curve''s proportions relative to how a LARGER body''s actual contours differ from the base size — point-specific grading (moving each control point of the curve by its own calculated amount, based on real anthropometric progression data) is needed to keep the curve correctly shaped/proportioned at every size, not just correctly SIZED overall', 'This directly extends the earlier pattern-grading and princess-seam discussions together — since princess seams (unlike simple straight seams) directly encode complex, multi-point body CONTOUR information (the specific curvature reflecting bust/waist/hip shape), and different body dimensions grow at genuinely different RATES across a size range (the core grade-rule insight), a uniform percentage scale-up would distort the curve''s proportional SHAPE, not just its size — potentially creating a poorly-fitting or visually awkward curve at larger/smaller sizes even if overall garment dimensions are technically correct — this is exactly why professional grading of curved, contour-sensitive seams requires point-specific grade rules derived from real body measurement progression data, more sophisticated than what a simple straight-seam garment requires.', 3, '{"princess-seam-grading-complexity"}'::text[]),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment factory''s industrial engineer calculates that switching a specific high-volume style''s collar-attaching operation from a single-needle lockstitch setup to a specialized collar-attaching machine (with automated folding/guiding attachments) would reduce that operation''s SAM from 1.8 minutes to 0.9 minutes. If this operation currently has 8 operators on a line producing at the operation''s current bottleneck-limited rate, and the line''s OTHER operations are not bottlenecked at this SAM level, what is the most likely immediate effect of this single-operation improvement on OVERALL line output, assuming operator count at this station is correspondingly reduced to match the new lower SAM requirement?', '["Overall line output would double immediately, since this operation''s SAM was cut exactly in half","Overall line output improvement depends entirely on whether this collar-attaching operation was ACTUALLY the line''s bottleneck — if another operation elsewhere on the line has an even higher SAM (making IT the true bottleneck), improving the collar operation (even by half its SAM) would deliver ZERO improvement to overall line output, since the line''s output remains capped by whatever operation is now the SLOWEST, directly applying the earlier Theory-of-Constraints principle","This improvement is guaranteed to increase overall line output by SOME meaningful amount, in direct proportion to the SAM reduction, regardless of any other operation''s SAM on the line","SAM reduction at any single operation always has zero effect on overall line output under any circumstances, making industrial engineering improvements at the individual-operation level pointless by definition"]'::jsonb, 'Overall line output improvement depends entirely on whether this collar-attaching operation was ACTUALLY the line''s bottleneck — if another operation elsewhere on the line has an even higher SAM (making IT the true bottleneck), improving the collar operation (even by half its SAM) would deliver ZERO improvement to overall line output, since the line''s output remains capped by whatever operation is now the SLOWEST, directly applying the earlier Theory-of-Constraints principle', 'This is a direct, rigorous application of the earlier Theory-of-Constraints principle to a realistic industrial engineering decision — a genuinely impressive-sounding 50% SAM reduction at ONE specific operation delivers ZERO overall line output benefit if that operation wasn''t actually the TRUE bottleneck (i.e., if some OTHER operation already has an equal or higher SAM) — this is exactly the kind of costly real-world mistake the earlier line-balancing/bottleneck discussion warned against: investing real engineering effort and capital (a specialized machine purchase) into improving a NON-bottleneck operation yields no overall throughput gain, reinforcing that correctly IDENTIFYING the true current bottleneck must always precede any improvement investment decision, regardless of how impressive that improvement looks in isolation.', 3, '{"bottleneck-improvement-verification"}'::text[]),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A technical designer explains that a garment''s ''ease'' allowance should NOT be applied UNIFORMLY across every body measurement point (e.g., adding the exact same 5cm ease to chest, waist, AND hip circumference). Why does sophisticated pattern-making instead apply DIFFERENT ease amounts to different measurement points, even within the SAME garment style?', '["Ease should always be applied as an identical uniform amount across every measurement point, making differentiated ease allocation an unnecessary, overly complex practice with zero fit benefit","Different body areas have genuinely different movement/comfort requirements and different aesthetic fit goals within the SAME garment — e.g., a fitted-waist, looser-hip silhouette requires LESS ease at the waist and MORE ease at the hip, even within one single style, reflecting the deliberate SILHOUETTE/fit design intent rather than a simple uniform ''add X cm everywhere'' rule, similar in principle to how princess seams encode point-specific body contour information rather than a single uniform scaling factor","Ease allowance has no relationship to garment silhouette design and is purely a manufacturing tolerance concept unrelated to any deliberate fit/style intent","Uniform ease application across all measurement points is mathematically the ONLY way to achieve a properly fitting garment, making differentiated ease allocation a fundamentally flawed pattern-making approach"]'::jsonb, 'Different body areas have genuinely different movement/comfort requirements and different aesthetic fit goals within the SAME garment — e.g., a fitted-waist, looser-hip silhouette requires LESS ease at the waist and MORE ease at the hip, even within one single style, reflecting the deliberate SILHOUETTE/fit design intent rather than a simple uniform ''add X cm everywhere'' rule, similar in principle to how princess seams encode point-specific body contour information rather than a single uniform scaling factor', 'This extends the earlier ease-allowance discussion with a more sophisticated, point-specific application principle — a single garment style can deliberately have DIFFERENT ease amounts at different body points to achieve a specific intended SILHOUETTE (e.g., a fit-and-flare dress wants minimal waist ease but generous hip/skirt ease), reflecting genuine design intent rather than a simplistic uniform allowance — this mirrors the earlier point-specific-grading insight (different body points need different treatment, not a single uniform factor) applied to ease allocation within a single size, rather than across a size range.', 3, '{"differentiated-ease-allocation"}'::text[]),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality engineer investigating a chronic ''puckered seam'' complaint on a specific fabric discovers the puckering DISAPPEARS when the SAME fabric is sewn at a SLOWER machine speed, but REAPPEARS at the factory''s standard production speed. Given everything established about seam pucker causes, what does this speed-dependence suggest about the root cause?', '["Machine speed has no possible causal relationship to seam pucker, making this observed pattern purely coincidental with no diagnostic value","This pattern points toward a DYNAMIC (speed-dependent) cause rather than a purely static one — likely differential fabric FEEDING between the top and bottom plies becoming more pronounced at higher speed (since feed-related mismatches often worsen with speed due to increased momentum/inertia effects on fabric handling), or thread tension dynamics that only destabilize at higher sewing speeds — ruling out purely static causes (like a fundamentally incompatible needle size, which would show puckering at ANY speed) and pointing toward a speed-sensitive mechanical/feeding-related root cause specifically","This pattern proves the fabric itself has an inherent structural pucker defect completely unrelated to the sewing process, contradicting the finding that slower speed eliminates the puckering","Puckering caused by thread tension is always speed-independent by definition, making this observed speed-sensitivity evidence AGAINST tension as a possible contributing cause"]'::jsonb, 'This pattern points toward a DYNAMIC (speed-dependent) cause rather than a purely static one — likely differential fabric FEEDING between the top and bottom plies becoming more pronounced at higher speed (since feed-related mismatches often worsen with speed due to increased momentum/inertia effects on fabric handling), or thread tension dynamics that only destabilize at higher sewing speeds — ruling out purely static causes (like a fundamentally incompatible needle size, which would show puckering at ANY speed) and pointing toward a speed-sensitive mechanical/feeding-related root cause specifically', 'This is an advanced diagnostic exercise extending the earlier seam-pucker-causes discussion — the specific observation that speed REDUCTION eliminates the defect is itself powerful diagnostic information: it rules OUT purely static/speed-independent causes (like fundamentally wrong needle size or fabric structural pucker, which would appear regardless of speed) and instead points toward DYNAMIC causes that worsen specifically at higher speed — differential ply feeding rates (top vs. bottom fabric layers feeding at slightly different rates, an effect that can compound with speed/momentum) or thread tension system dynamics that only become unstable at higher operating speeds — correctly using this speed-sensitivity clue narrows the root-cause investigation much faster than treating all the earlier-established pucker causes as equally likely.', 3, '{"speed-dependent-pucker-diagnosis"}'::text[]);
